RUNNING THE ANALYSIS
====================

This file describes how to reproduce the experimental-data and simulated
held-out-data analyses distributed with this repository.

All commands below are intended to be run from the top-level repository
directory:

    Static_ML_Vertex_Reconstruction/

For example:

    cd /data/grgroup/gr02/bmeleton/Static_ML_Vertex_Reconstruction


----------------------------------------------------------------------
1. IMPORTANT NOTES ABOUT THE DISTRIBUTED DATA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository does not contain every raw file used during the original
analysis. Large upstream files have been omitted where a smaller derived
data product is sufficient to reproduce the publication analysis.

The repository is intended to reproduce:

  1. Experimental static-ML candidate generation from the distributed
     experimental Hough-line outputs.

  2. Application of the trained four-dataset static-ML vertex model to
     the experimental candidates.

  3. Experimental center-of-mass reconstruction using the distributed
     reconstructed point clouds and alpha-energy table.

  4. Comparison of the static-ML and Hough vertices with the common SRIM
     stopping curve.

  5. Application of the trained static-ML model to the independent
     simulated 30% held-out sample.

  6. The simulated ML-versus-oracle performance summary.

  7. The four-dataset 4 x 3 dx/dy/dz residual figure.


The following large upstream data are NOT included.


A. Original experimental detector data
------------------------------------------------------

The original experimental data detector files used to reconstruct the
outgoing alpha-particle kinetic energy are not distributed with this
repository.

These files are large and are not required for the downstream analysis
because the derived alpha-energy table is included:

    experimental_data/experimental_data_analysis/
        alpha_energy_reconstructed/alpha_kinetic_energy.csv

The script

    scripts/reconstruct_alpha_kinetic_energy_py36_offsets_static_vertices.py

is retained for transparency and for users who have access to the
original files. However, the original point clouds and hough lines for all the 
experimental data used ARE included here.

B. Original simulated ROOT truth files
---------------------------------------

The original TeBATSim ROOT files are not distributed because of their
size.

The ROOT files were required when the simulated Hough-line candidates
were first converted into truth-labeled static candidate feature tables.
In particular, the simulation truth vertex is required to construct
quantities such as:

    dist_candidate_minus_truth
    dx_candidate_minus_truth
    dy_candidate_minus_truth
    dz_candidate_minus_truth

These truth quantities are required for training and for the held-out
oracle/residual analysis.

The truth-labeled 70% training and 30% held-out feature tables are
therefore distributed directly:

    simulated_data/simulated_collected_features/train_70/
        static_candidate_features_combined_train70_generic.csv

    simulated_data/simulated_collected_features/test_30/
        static_candidate_features_combined_test30_generic.csv

Because the simulated ROOT truth files are not included, the public
no-ROOT simulation workflow begins from these feature tables rather
than from the original simulated Hough-line files.


C. Original simulated Hough-line and point-cloud collections
-------------------------------------------------------------

The full simulated Hough-line and reconstructed point-cloud collections
are also not required for the distributed held-out validation workflow
and are not included in this repository.

The retained truth-labeled feature tables contain the candidate
information needed to train, apply, and validate the static-ML model.

The experimental point clouds ARE included because they are required by
the center-of-mass reconstruction to refit the beam, alpha, and recoil
track directions. Additionally, the number of experimental data events 
is significantly smaller than the number of simulated events used for 
training.


